window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-b0e7a45d11db50af"]={"zh":"<p><a id=\"manage-creative-requests-from-brief-to-delivery\"></a></p> <h1 id=\"_1\">从需求简报到交付，全程管理创意请求</h1> <p>收集完整的需求简报、审核草稿，并将最终素材与每项请求关联保存。</p> <p>将创意需求模板转换为请求应用：同事可以提交工作，设计人员可以管理队列，评审者可以针对草稿提供反馈。</p> <p><a id=\"best-for\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_2\">适合哪些情况</h2> <ul> <li>通过零散消息接收设计请求的小型创意团队。</li> <li>需要协调营销活动素材和审核期限的营销团队。</li> </ul> <p><a id=\"starter-prompt\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_3\">起步提示词</h2> <p>从需求简报到交付，全程管理创意请求</p> <p>建议推理级别：<code>medium</code></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">使用 @Sites，根据我们的需求简报模板为[team]（团队）构建创意请求应用。\n\n资料来源：\n- 需求简报模板：[attached file or document link]（附件或文档链接）。\n- 品牌指南和已批准素材：[document or folder links]（文档或文件夹链接）。\n- 现有请求（如有）：[spreadsheet or none]（电子表格或无）。\n- 创意团队成员和评审者：[supplied list]（提供的名单）。\n\n先确认你能读取哪些资料，并总结必填字段。询问缺少的负责人、含糊的日期或无法访问的来源。仅使用提供的材料；不要编造品牌规则或真实请求。\n\n构建需求表单，包含标题、受众、核心信息、格式或尺寸、需求日期、评审者，以及文案或参考链接。根据[request types, such as event graphics or presentations]（请求类型，例如活动图片或演示文稿）调整字段。提供字段示例，并说明如何修正缺失或无效的内容。将需求日期与创意团队商定的交付日期分开。\n\n为每项请求创建详情页，包含需求简报、请求人、负责人、评审者、商定日期、状态、草稿链接、反馈和最终素材链接。状态使用“新建”“处理中”“审核中”和“已交付”。让最新草稿与已交付素材容易区分。添加请求队列，并提供负责人、状态和商定交付日期筛选器。让未分配负责人和缺少商定日期的请求清晰可见。\n\n要求用户登录。允许请求人创建并查看自己的请求、添加反馈；只有指定的创意团队可以管理所有请求、分配任务并更新交付状态。在服务器端执行访问规则，包括直接访问记录时的权限检查。在不同访问之间保存记录和反馈。\n\n先使用经过审核的请求数据副本，并链接到原始素材。说明哪些内容会被复制、哪些内容仍以链接形式提供。不要暗示源文件会自动同步，也不要修改已连接的文档或任务跟踪器。\n\n使用标明为虚构的请求进行测试。测试必填字段、提交、状态更改、重新加载后反馈是否保存，以及两个请求人是否拥有不同访问权限。检查手机布局。将网站设为仅对工作区开放，并在发布前展示已保存的预览、测试结果和未解决的问题。\n</code></pre> <p><a id=\"related-links\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_4\">关联文档</h2> <ul> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-e0f092cc982945c2\">数据与连接</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-fc9cd7880ec6ce59\">发布与分享</a></li> </ul> <blockquote> <p>如需完整文档索引，请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。在页面 URL 末尾追加 <code>.md</code>，即可获取该页面的 Markdown 版本。</p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"before-you-start\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_5\">开始之前</h2> <p>为同事提供一个统一入口来提交创意工作请求，并跟踪审核和交付进度。请求应用可以收集设计人员需要的信息、显示由谁负责工作，并将反馈与最新草稿放在一起。请求人无需翻找消息就能查看进度；创意团队也能看到整个队列中哪些任务需要处理。</p> <p>选择一种经常出现的请求，例如活动图片、演示文稿或营销活动素材。收集团队已经在使用的材料：</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>资料来源</th> <th>示例</th> <th>可提供的内容</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>请求简报</td> <td>包含受众、核心信息、尺寸和需求日期的表单或文档</td> <td>必填字段和实用示例</td> </tr> <tr> <td>品牌指南</td> <td>团队共享盘中的已批准文档</td> <td>设计规范以及已批准素材的链接</td> </tr> <tr> <td>当前请求列表</td> <td>包含请求、负责人、评审者、状态和交付日期的电子表格</td> <td>起始记录；如果想把现有工作导入门户时使用</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>确定由谁提交请求、由谁分配工作，以及由谁批准结果。请在提示词中说明这些角色，让应用符合团队的工作方式。将需求日期与创意团队商定的交付日期分开。</p> <p><a id=\"example-a-webinar-graphic\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_6\">示例：网络研讨会图片</h3> <p>在这个假设性示例中，营销人员需要一张用于运营负责人主题网络研讨会的 1200 × 628 图片。需求简报包含已批准的标题、注册链接、评审者和期望的交付日期。设计人员接受请求、商定日期并添加草稿。评审者在该请求下提供反馈；素材准备好后，设计人员再添加最终版本。</p> <p>同一流程也适用于其他交付物。请按工作类型调整表单字段：演示文稿可能需要幻灯片数量和演讲者备注；印刷素材可能需要成品尺寸和制作截止时间。</p> <p><a id=\"connect-your-sources\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_7\">连接资料来源</h2> <ol> <li>在构建对话中附上需求简报模板和一份小型请求数据导出文件。如果要使用已连接应用中的文档，请询问 ChatGPT 当前有哪些连接可用，然后说出应用名称并提供准确的文档标题或链接。完成 ChatGPT 要求的任何连接步骤。例如，如果可以使用 Google Drive，请提供“Brand guidelines”文档和“Approved assets”文件夹的链接，并询问它能读取哪些材料。</li> <li>让 ChatGPT 找出必填字段、总结品牌指南，并说明电子表格中的列如何对应到请求记录。导入现有记录之前，先补齐负责人或日期信息。</li> <li>在应用中保存需求简报、状态更新和反馈，并链接到原始文案、草稿和最终素材。将电子表格复制到网站中并不会让网站与原文件保持同步。</li> <li>如果希望访客能够获取最新的品牌资源，请为已批准的文件夹或文档请求受支持的只读连接。将网站设为仅对工作区开放，并使用队友自己的账户和权限进行测试。参阅<a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/codex/sites/data-and-connections#build-a-site-with-connected-data\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">数据与连接</a>（官网章节，需联网） <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/codex/sites/data-and-connections\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">本地页面（本地尚未收录）</a>。</li> </ol> <p>如果某种连接不可用，请附上需求简报模板和请求数据导出文件，并链接到原始素材。确认目标评审者能打开链接的文件。除非添加集成，否则应用中的请求状态与其他应用中的任务是彼此独立的记录。</p> <p><a id=\"how-to-use-it\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_8\">使用方法</h2> <ol> <li>提交设计人员可以直接使用的简报</li> </ol> <p>使用起始提示词和资料来源，然后提交一项虚构请求。从实际执行工作的人员角度阅读生成的简报：能否看出素材面向谁、要传达什么、哪些文案已获批准，以及由谁评审？如果某个字段过于含糊，请让 ChatGPT 提出更具体的问题，或在字段下方添加示例。</p> <p>试着将一个必填字段留空。确认表单会说明缺少什么、保留其他已填内容，并且提交时只会创建一项请求。</p> <ol> <li>审核草稿并交付素材</li> </ol> <p>指定负责人并商定交付日期。添加草稿链接，将请求状态改为“审核中”，再以请求人的身份填写反馈。对于示例图片，反馈可以是：“保留标题和图片。让活动日期在电子邮件中的显示尺寸下更容易阅读。”具体的反馈能让设计人员清楚下一步该做什么。</p> <p>添加最终素材链接并将请求标记为“已交付”。重新打开请求，检查需求简报、反馈和更新是否仍然保留。之后再次查看的人应该能区分已交付素材和较早的草稿。</p> <ol> <li>与合适的人员共享请求队列</li> </ol> <p>按负责人、状态和商定交付日期筛选队列。用它查看即将到期的工作，以及哪些请求正在等待评审者回复。未分配的请求或缺少商定日期的请求都应该清晰可见，方便团队跟进。</p> <p>使用单独的请求人账户和创意团队账户进行测试。请求人应只能看到自己的请求；创意团队则应能够管理整个队列。确认其他请求人不能通过直接打开 URL 来读取或更改请求。检查网站受众范围，然后为团队发布。若团队反复遇到同一种信息缺失，可以相应调整表单。</p> <p><a id=\"take-it-further\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_9\">进一步优化</h2> <p><a id=\"load-brand-resources-from-a-connected-app\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_10\">从已连接的应用加载品牌资源</h3> <p>使用受支持的连接展示每位访客可以访问的品牌指南，并提供指向原始文档的链接。</p> <p><strong>提示词：</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Check which of my connected apps can read [approved brand folder or document links]. If supported, add a Brand resources section that retrieves the relevant guidelines with the visitor's own connection and links to the originals. If access is missing, identify the resource that couldn't be loaded and explain how the visitor can connect their account or request access. Keep request briefs and feedback stored in the app, and don't change the source documents. If the connection isn't available, use the supplied resource links and explain what needs manual updating.\n</code></pre><div class=\"prompt-translation\"><div class=\"prompt-reference-heading\"><strong>提示词中文参考</strong><button class=\"copy-code copy-prompt-translation\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"复制中文参考\">复制中文</button></div><div class=\"prompt-reference-text\">请检查我已连接的哪些应用可以读取 [approved brand folder or document links]。如果支持，添加一个“品牌资源”区域，使用访问者自己的连接获取相关指南，并链接到原始资料。如果缺少访问权限，指出无法加载的资源，并说明访问者如何连接账户或申请访问权限。将需求简报和反馈保存在应用中，不要更改来源文档。如果连接不可用，请使用所提供的资源链接，并说明哪些内容需要手动更新。</div></div> <p><a id=\"find-overdue-requests-and-missing-feedback\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_11\">找出逾期请求和缺少反馈的请求</h3> <p>添加一个视图，将逾期请求、等待反馈的请求，以及没有商定交付日期的请求分别显示。</p> <p><strong>提示词：</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Add a Needs attention view for requests awaiting feedback or past their agreed delivery date. Show the request, owner, reviewer, requested date, and agreed date. Keep requests without an agreed date in a separate group. Preserve the existing access rules. Test an overdue request, one waiting for feedback, and one without an agreed date; don't send reminders automatically.\n</code></pre><div class=\"prompt-translation\"><div class=\"prompt-reference-heading\"><strong>提示词中文参考</strong><button class=\"copy-code copy-prompt-translation\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"复制中文参考\">复制中文</button></div><div class=\"prompt-reference-text\">请添加一个“需要关注”视图，列出等待反馈或超过约定交付日期的请求。展示请求、负责人、审阅者、请求日期和约定日期。将没有约定日期的请求放在单独一组。保持现有访问规则。测试一个已逾期的请求、一个等待反馈的请求，以及一个没有约定日期的请求；不要自动发送提醒。</div></div>","en":"<h1 id=\"manage-creative-requests-from-brief-to-delivery\">Manage creative requests from brief to delivery</h1> <p>Collect complete briefs, review drafts, and keep final assets with each request.</p> <p>Turn your creative brief template into a request app where colleagues submit work, designers manage the queue, and reviewers give feedback on drafts.</p> <h2 id=\"best-for\">Best for</h2> <ul> <li>Small creative teams receiving design requests through scattered messages.</li> <li>Marketing teams coordinating campaign assets and review deadlines.</li> </ul> <h2 id=\"starter-prompt\">Starter prompt</h2> <p>Manage creative requests from brief to delivery</p> <p>Suggested reasoning effort：<code>medium</code></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Use @Sites to build a creative request app for [team] using our brief template.\n\nSources:\n- Brief template: [attached file or document link].\n- Brand guidelines and approved assets: [document or folder links].\n- Existing requests, if any: [spreadsheet or none].\n- Creative-team members and reviewers: [supplied list].\n\nFirst confirm which sources you can read and summarize the required fields. Ask about missing owners, unclear dates, or unavailable sources. Use the supplied materials; don't invent brand rules or real requests.\n\nBuild an intake form for title, audience, key message, format or dimensions, requested date, reviewer, and copy or reference links. Adapt the fields to [request types, such as event graphics or presentations]. Show field examples and explain how to fix missing or invalid entries. Keep the requested date separate from the delivery date agreed by the creative team.\n\nGive each request a detail page with its brief, requester, owner, reviewer, agreed date, status, draft link, feedback, and final asset link. Use New, In progress, In review, and Delivered. Make the latest draft and delivered asset easy to distinguish. Add a queue with owner, status, and agreed-delivery-date filters. Make unassigned requests and missing agreed dates visible.\n\nRequire sign-in. Let requesters create and read their own requests and add feedback. Let only the named creative team manage all requests, assignment, and delivery status. Enforce access on the server, including direct record access. Save records and feedback between visits.\n\nStart with a reviewed copy of supplied request data and links to original assets. Explain which content is copied and which remains linked. Don't imply that source files stay synchronized, and don't modify connected documents or task trackers.\n\nUse labeled fictional requests for testing. Test required fields, submission, status changes, saved feedback after reload, and two requesters with different access. Check the layout on mobile. Keep the site private to our workspace and show a saved preview, test results, and any unresolved questions before publishing.\n</code></pre> <h2 id=\"related-links\">Related links</h2> <ul> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-e0f092cc982945c2\">Data and connections</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-fc9cd7880ec6ce59\">Publish and share</a></li> </ul> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote> <h2 id=\"before-you-start\">Before you start</h2> <p>Give colleagues one place to request creative work and follow it through review and delivery. A request app can collect the details a designer needs, show who owns the work, and keep feedback beside the latest draft. The requester can check progress without searching through messages; the creative team can see what needs attention across its queue.</p> <p>Choose a recurring request, such as an event graphic, presentation, or campaign asset. Gather the material you already use:</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Source</th> <th>Example</th> <th>What it contributes</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Request brief</td> <td>A form or document with audience, message, dimensions, and requested date</td> <td>Required fields and helpful examples</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Brand guidelines</td> <td>An approved document in your team's shared drive</td> <td>Design guidance and links to approved assets</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Current request list</td> <td>A spreadsheet with request, owner, reviewer, status, and delivery date</td> <td>Starting records, if you want to bring existing work into the portal</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Decide who submits requests, who assigns the work, and who approves the result. Include those roles in your prompt so the app reflects how your team works. Keep a requested date separate from the delivery date the creative team agrees to.</p> <h3 id=\"example-a-webinar-graphic\">Example: A webinar graphic</h3> <p>In this hypothetical example, a marketer needs a 1200 × 628 graphic for a webinar aimed at operations leaders. The brief includes the approved headline, registration link, reviewer, and requested delivery date. A designer accepts the request, agrees on a date, and adds a draft. The reviewer leaves feedback on that request, and the designer adds the final asset when it's ready.</p> <p>You can use the same flow for another deliverable. Change the form fields to match the work: a presentation might need a slide count and speaker notes, while a print asset might need print dimensions and a production deadline.</p> <h2 id=\"connect-your-sources\">Connect your sources</h2> <ol> <li>Attach the brief template and a small request export to the build conversation. To use a document from a connected app, ask ChatGPT which connections are available, then name the app and provide the exact document title or link. Complete any connection step ChatGPT requests. For example, if Google Drive is available, provide the link to your “Brand guidelines” document and “Approved assets” folder, and ask which materials it can read.</li> <li>Ask ChatGPT to identify the required fields, summarize the brand guidance, and show how spreadsheet columns map to request records. Resolve missing owners or unclear dates before importing records.</li> <li>Save briefs, status updates, and feedback in the app. Link to original copy, drafts, and finished assets. Copying a spreadsheet into the site doesn't keep it synchronized with the original.</li> <li>If you want visitors to retrieve current brand resources, ask for a supported read connection to the approved folder or documents. Keep the site private to your workspace and test with a teammate's own account and permissions. See <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/codex/sites/data-and-connections#build-a-site-with-connected-data\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">data and connections</a> (official section, online) <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/codex/sites/data-and-connections\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Local page (not in this local snapshot)</a>.</li> </ol> <p>If a connection isn't available, attach the brief template and any request export, and link to the original assets. Check that the intended reviewers can open the linked files. The app's request status and a task in another app are separate records unless you add an integration.</p> <h2 id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it</h2> <ol> <li>Submit a brief a designer can use</li> </ol> <p>Use the starter prompt with your sources, then submit a fictional request. Read the resulting brief as the person doing the work: can you tell who the asset is for, what it should communicate, which copy is approved, and who will review it? If a field is too vague, ask ChatGPT for a more specific question or an example beneath it.</p> <p>Try leaving a required field blank. Check that the form explains what is missing, retains the other entries, and creates only one request when submitted.</p> <ol> <li>Review the draft and deliver the asset</li> </ol> <p>Assign an owner and agree on a delivery date. Add a draft link, move the request to In review, and leave feedback as the requester. For the sample graphic, feedback might be: “Keep the headline and image. Make the event date easier to read at the size used in an email.” Specific feedback gives the designer a clear next action.</p> <p>Add the final asset link and mark the request Delivered. Reopen it to check that the brief, feedback, and updates remain. Someone returning later should be able to distinguish the delivered asset from an earlier draft.</p> <ol> <li>Share the queue with the right people</li> </ol> <p>Filter the queue by owner, status, and agreed delivery date. Use it to see which work is due next and which requests need a reviewer’s response. An unassigned request or a missing agreed date should be visible so the team can resolve it.</p> <p>Test with separate requester and creative-team accounts. A requester should see their own requests; the creative team should manage the queue. Confirm that another requester can't read or change a request by opening its URL directly. Review the site's audience, then publish it for your team. Adjust the form when the same missing detail keeps coming up.</p> <h2 id=\"take-it-further\">Take it further</h2> <h3 id=\"load-brand-resources-from-a-connected-app\">Load brand resources from a connected app</h3> <p>Use a supported connection to show brand guidelines each visitor can access, with links to the original documents.</p> <p><strong>Prompt:</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Check which of my connected apps can read [approved brand folder or document links]. If supported, add a Brand resources section that retrieves the relevant guidelines with the visitor's own connection and links to the originals. If access is missing, identify the resource that couldn't be loaded and explain how the visitor can connect their account or request access. Keep request briefs and feedback stored in the app, and don't change the source documents. If the connection isn't available, use the supplied resource links and explain what needs manual updating.\n</code></pre> <h3 id=\"find-overdue-requests-and-missing-feedback\">Find overdue requests and missing feedback</h3> <p>Add a view that separates overdue requests, requests waiting for feedback, and requests without an agreed delivery date.</p> <p><strong>Prompt:</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Add a Needs attention view for requests awaiting feedback or past their agreed delivery date. Show the request, owner, reviewer, requested date, and agreed date. Keep requests without an agreed date in a separate group. Preserve the existing access rules. Test an overdue request, one waiting for feedback, and one without an agreed date; don't send reminders automatically.\n</code></pre>"};
